From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Derek Ross <derekross@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 12:06:51 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 06/12] software_isp: Focus on the frame centre, with
 statistics every frame

The contrast autofocus measured the sharpness over the whole statistics
window, got statistics only every fourth frame, and drove the lens on
every frame of every camera with a lens, whether the tuning file enabled
autofocus or not.

- Measure the sharpness over the centre half of the window in each
  direction, where the subject usually is, and normalize it by the green
  samples there.
- Gather statistics every frame when the camera has a focus lens, so a
  scan does not take four times as long; cameras without a lens keep one
  set every four frames. The exposure loop works from the sensor values
  each frame was taken with, so more frequent statistics do not make it
  step twice for one change.
- Send the focus position only while autofocus runs and only when it
  changes, and report no autofocus metadata without a lens.
- Take the scan's coarse and fine step counts and an optional part of
  the lens range from the tuning file (coarseSteps, fineSteps, range).

A follow-up by Robin Snyders did the first two with a 5x5 grid
of statistics and the AfWindows control; a fixed centre window costs a
compare per sample instead of a division.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
